<title>NETHERLANDS: Dutch bonds give up most of their gains by close.</title>
<headline>Dutch bonds give up most of their gains by close.</headline>
<text>
<p>---------------------(Snapshot at 1545 GMT)---------------------</p>
<p>* DUTCH STATE LOANS	 PRICES			  BID YIELDS</p>
<p>  June 1999 7.50 pct	106.73/80 (-0.03)	   3.76 (+0.01)</p>
<p>  Feb  2002		   114.81/90 (-0.02)	   4.57 (unch.)</p>
<p>  Feb  2007 5.75 pct	101.45/60 (+0.04)	   5.55 (unch.)</p>
<p>  Jan  2023		   114.68/90 (+0.15)	   6.32 (-0.01)</p>
<p>* BUND FUTURE (Sept)	102.46    (+0.14)</p>
<p>  TREASURIES  (Sept)	114.41 (+0.47 vs pvs Dutch close)</p>
<p>* DM/NLG			1.1259 (prev 1.1259)</p>
<p>--------------------------(SPREADS)----------------------------</p>
<p>*DSLs versus Bunds  (pvs close)    *DSL curve     (pvs close)</p>
<p> 5 yr	 +0.03	+0.02	  10-2 yr  179     (181)</p>
<p> 10 yr	-0.06	-0.07	  10-5 yr   98	(98)</p>
<p> Long	 -0.06	-0.07</p>
<p>----------------------------------------------------------------</p>
<p>* Dutch bonds gave up most of their earlier gains by the close as the strong dollar continued to cap the upside. Some dealers predict European central banks to intervene in the short term and to hike interest rates later this year to curb the dollar's rise. Yield spreads versus Germany narrow slightly.</p>
